{"answer":"The ledger will be handled by a designated ledger‑keeper.  We’ll choose that person by a simple rotation – every month a different fisher is voted in by the community (or drawn by lot if we’re feeling democratic).  Each day the keeper takes the ledger from the dock, records the hauls that have just finished, and posts the totals for that day.  If nobody volunteers on a particular day the next person in the rotation steps in.  This keeps the bookkeeping honest, distributes the work, and gives everyone a chance to stay involved.

revised_policy":"Each fisher may keep up to 12 kg per trip, but must donate 1 kg to a communal reserve that supports lake maintenance and emergency feeding. At the end of each trip, fishers report their haul to a community ledger. Those who exceed 12 kg are required to give 1 kg to the reserve. The reserve is collected monthly and used for fish stocking, gear repair, or as a safety net for any fisher with depleted reserves. Failure to donate 1 kg results in a 2 kg deduction from the next haul.","revised_operationalization":"Daily: The ledger‑keeper (chosen by rotation each month) takes the communal ledger to the dock each morning, records all hauls from the previous day, and updates the running total of the communal reserve. Monthly: The community reviews the ledger, decides how the reserve will be allocated, and records that decision in the ledger. All fishers must sign their entries. The ledger keeper is also responsible for maintaining the ledger’s physical security and ensuring it is kept in good condition.

reasoning":"I chose a rotating ledger‑keeper to prevent power concentration and to give every fisher a stake in the bookkeeping.  A monthly community vote or random draw keeps the process fair and transparent, while daily responsibility ensures the ledger stays up to date.  This approach fits the communal nature of the rule and respects each fisher’s autonomy."}

{"answer":"Council verifies the deposit by using a shared deposit station with a weigh‑scale and a sealed reserve bucket. After a fisher weighs his haul, he physically drops the required 1 kg (or the whole haul if under 1 kg) into the bucket. The bucket is then weighed immediately by a council officer or a calibrated sensor; if the weight matches the ledger entry, the deposit is confirmed. The officer signs a short receipt and the fisher updates the communal ledger. The bucket is kept in a visible area so all fishers can see the total reserve at any time, and the council audits the bucket’s weight against the ledger monthly. This ensures that a deposit has indeed been made before the ledger entry is recorded.

I keep my original proposal because the verification process simply adds a physical confirmation step before the ledger update. The policy itself remains unchanged.

", "revised_policy":"Each fisher may keep up to 14 kg per trip. The fisher must donate exactly 1 kg to the communal reserve. If the haul exceeds 14 kg, the excess beyond 14 kg is also donated. If a fisher’s haul is less than 1 kg, the fisher must donate the entire haul to the reserve and keep none. After each trip, the fisher weighs the haul, sets aside the required 1 kg (or the whole haul if under 1 kg), and immediately records the deposit in the communal reserve ledger. The ledger is visible to all fishers and is updated instantly after every deposit. The community council audits the ledger and confirms the totals during the monthly council meeting. Failure to record a required deposit results in a one‑cycle fishing ban until compliance is restored.", "revised_operationalization":"After each trip, the fisher weighs his haul on the communal scale, separates the mandatory 1 kg (or the entire haul if less than 1 kg) and deposits it into the communal reserve bucket. A council officer then re‑weighs the bucket to confirm the deposit, signs a receipt, and the fisher updates the ledger immediately. The reserve bucket is kept in a common area so all fishers can view the current reserve balance. The council audits the bucket’s total weight against the ledger each month.", "reasoning":"I added a concrete verification step—using a communal weigh‑scale and a sealed bucket—so the council can confirm deposits before the ledger is updated. This keeps the policy unchanged while ensuring compliance."}

{"answer":"The ban is handled by the community monitor – the same person who weighs the lake each Sunday. When a fisher is caught exceeding the limits, the monitor writes the one‑day ban next to the fisher’s name on the shared ledger. The fisher is told in person that they cannot set out the next day, and the ban is also posted on the community notice board and announced at the following weigh‑in. The community fishers check the ledger before each trip; if a fisher is listed as banned, they simply won’t let them go out. No one else has extra power beyond the monitor – it’s a shared responsibility to uphold the ban.

The ban is recorded on the ledger so everyone can see it, communicated personally by the monitor, and displayed on the community board, ensuring transparency and that no one can fish that day.
","revised_policy":"No fisher may take more than 10% of the lake’s current stock per trip, capped at 10kg. The community’s total weekly catch must not exceed 20% of lake stock. A regeneration reserve of 10% of the lake must remain untouched. If the lake’s stock falls below 120kg, all fishing is paused until recovery. Once the weekly weigh‑in shows the stock is 120kg or higher, fishing may resume automatically. Each Sunday, the community monitor will weigh the lake, compute the 20% weekly quota and 10% reserve, post the figures on the shared ledger, and enforce the 10% trip limit and 20% weekly cap. If a fisher exceeds limits, the excess fish are returned and the fisher receives a one‑day ban.","revised_operationalization":"After weighing, the monitor posts the 10% trip limit, 20% weekly quota, 10% reserve, and lake stock on the shared ledger. If a fisher exceeds the limit, the monitor records a one‑day ban on the ledger next to the fisher’s name, announces the ban in person, and posts a notice on the community board. The ban is visible to all fishers and will be checked before any new trip is authorized. The ban is automatically lifted after one day, and the fisher may resume fishing only after the next Sunday weigh‑in confirms stock ≥120kg. Enforcement is by the community monitor and the collective respect of the community.","reasoning":"The ban must be enforceable by a neutral party (the monitor) and visible to all so that the community can collectively respect it. Recording it on the ledger ensures transparency and prevents any fisher from sneaking around."}

{"answer":"The rule is clear: the fisher who caught the excess is the one who must return it to the lake. If the total kept by everyone on a given day tops 10 kg, Kai will tally the totals. The portion of each fisher’s catch that pushes the total over the 10‑kg cap must be released by that fisher under Kai’s supervision. Kai will then update the communal ledger by subtracting that excess from the kept amount and marking the day’s total as “voided” (with a note of the adjustment). Anyone who logs more than 3 kg in a trip is required to record the surplus as released immediately, and they cannot fish again that day. This keeps the system fair and the ledger accurate.

We keep the policy as written, with a small clarification added for the voided‑day case.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may keep a maximum of 3 kg per trip, and the community shall enforce a total daily catch cap of 10 kg. Excess catch must be released immediately by the fisher who caught it. If the sum of kept fish exceeds 10 kg, the day’s totals are voided, and any excess must be released by the fishers who contributed to the excess under Kai’s supervision. The ledger will be adjusted to reflect the voided totals by reducing each fisher’s kept amount accordingly, with a note indicating the adjustment. Any fisher who logs a catch above 3 kg must immediately note the surplus as released and must not fish again until the next day. Kai performs rotating spot‑checks to verify ledger entries; a confirmed violation results in a one‑day suspension and the fisher must release the excess from that day’s haul.","revised_operationalization":"If the daily total exceeds 10 kg, Kai will tally the catch after all trips. For each fisher who exceeded 3 kg, Kai will calculate the amount that pushed the day’s total over 10 kg and instruct that fisher to release that surplus immediately. Kai will then adjust the ledger by subtracting the excess from the kept amount and marking the day’s totals as voided. All released fish are returned to the lake. If a fisher logs a catch over 3 kg, they must note the surplus as released in the ledger and will not fish again that day. Kai will perform spot‑checks; a confirmed violation leads to a one‑day suspension and the fisher must release the excess from that day’s haul.","reasoning":"I added the missing detail about who releases excess when the daily cap is breached and how the ledger is updated, while preserving the original policy’s intent and clarity. The answer stays in character, focusing on fairness and sustainability."}

{"answer":"The proposal as written explains how the keeper applies a 3‑kg quota cut when the lake falls below 120 kg, but it does not say how a keeper knows a fisher has *not* left the 5 kg reserve on a given trip, nor how a single or repeated failure translates into a quota adjustment. In practice the keeper must audit the reserve logged by each fisher against the lake’s daily tally. If a fisher’s logged reserve is < 5 kg or the lake total is lower than the expected amount (within the 0.5 kg tolerance) the keeper records a “Reserve Violation.” The policy does not yet state how many violations trigger a penalty. To close that gap I propose that two consecutive reserve violations (i.e., the fisher fails to leave 5 kg on two successive trips) will trigger the 3‑kg quota reduction for the next five trips, with the columns ‘Current Quota’, ‘Original Quota’, and ‘Reduced Trips Remaining’ added to that fisher’s row on the shared sheet. The keeper will then enforce the new quota and, once the reduced trips counter reaches zero, restore the original quota. This keeps the system clear and consistent with the rest of the rules.

The rest of the proposal remains unchanged.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take up to 12 kg of fish per trip as personal harvest. Of the fish caught, the fisher must leave 5 kg in the lake as a communal reserve. The 12 kg limit applies to the fish the fisher keeps; any catch beyond 12 kg (after reserving 5 kg) must be returned to the lake. The lake’s total must never fall below 120 kg; if it does, all quotas are cut by 3 kg for the next five trips. On every trip the fisher logs the total catch on a shared sheet. The fisher then sets aside 5 kg in the lake as a reserve, logging that. From the remaining catch the fisher may keep up to 12 kg; any fish beyond that 12 kg is returned to the lake and logged. A designated keeper tallies the lake’s total weight daily, signs, and publishes the record. If the keeper’s recorded weight differs by more than 0.5 kg from the sum of individual logs, the impartial observer verifies the discrepancy. If a fisher violates the reserve or quota rule, the keeper records a reduced quota for that fisher’s next trip on the shared sheet, signs it, and the fisher must adhere to the new quota. **Additionally, if a fisher fails to leave the required 5 kg reserve on a trip, the keeper will log a “Reserve Violation” on that fisher’s entry. Two consecutive reserve violations trigger the 3‑kg quota reduction for the next five trips, adding the columns ‘Current Quota’, ‘Original Quota’, and ‘Reduced Trips Remaining’ as described.**","revised_operationalization":"The keeper will maintain a daily log of the lake’s total weight. For each fisher, the keeper records the logged total catch, the 5 kg reserve set aside, and any fish kept up to 12 kg. The keeper checks that the reserve entry equals 5 kg; if not, a ‘Reserve Violation’ is recorded. If a fisher has two consecutive violations, the keeper reduces that fisher’s ‘Current Quota’ by 3 kg, sets ‘Reduced Trips Remaining’ to 5, and adds the three new columns. The keeper decrements the ‘Reduced Trips Remaining’ after each trip. When the counter reaches zero, the keeper resets ‘Current Quota’ to 12 kg and clears the reduced trips counter. All updates are signed and published; the impartial observer verifies discrepancies in the same way as before.","reasoning":"I added the missing definition of repeated reserve violations and the trigger for the quota cut, keeping all other aspects of the policy identical."}

{"answer":"When the ledger shows that the total for a week is higher than the agreed cap (110 kg or whatever the cap was adjusted to that week), the ledger automatically flags that week as ‘over‑quota’. The community then holds a short review: all fish caught that week are marked as excess. Each fisher whose cumulative catch in that week pushed the total past the cap gets a warning recorded. If the same fisher causes an over‑quota week again, the council votes for a one‑trip suspension (or, if the community prefers, a small fine equal to a set percentage of the excess weight). The excess fish are returned to the lake or otherwise forfeited so that the lake’s reserve reflects only the quota‑legal catch.

In short, the ledger’s flag triggers a community check, a warning is issued for the violator(s), and repeated violations trigger suspension or fines—making it clear that the cap is binding and enforceable.


","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 10 kg per trip, and the community’s total catch per week may not exceed 110 kg. No one may catch more than 20% of the weekly total. The weekly catch cap will be adjusted by ±10 kg if the lake reserve falls below 100 kg or rises above 200 kg. At the end of each week the community sums all catches from the shared ledger to determine the lake reserve. The council or fishery committee reviews the reserve and votes to adjust the weekly cap by ±10 kg if needed. Trips over 10 kg are flagged and incur warnings; repeated violations lead to a one‑trip suspension. If the weekly total catch exceeds the cap, the ledger flags the week as over‑quota. The community then reviews the excess; all fish caught that week are marked as excess, and each fisher who contributed to the over‑quota receives a warning. A second over‑quota week for the same fisher triggers a one‑trip suspension or a fine equal to a set percentage of the excess weight. The cap adjustments apply retroactively to the following week’s quota.","revised_operationalization":"The shared ledger automatically sums each week’s catches. If the sum exceeds the current cap, the ledger logs the week as over‑quota. The ledger issues a warning to every fisher whose cumulative catch that week pushed the total past the cap. If a fisher receives a second warning for an over‑quota week, the council votes to impose a one‑trip suspension or a fine (e.g., 5% of the excess weight). The excess fish are returned to the lake or forfeited, ensuring the lake reserve reflects only the quota‑legal catch. All warnings, suspensions, and fines are recorded in the ledger for transparency.","reasoning":"I added a clear penalty for exceeding the weekly cap because the original policy left that scenario unaddressed. By making the ledger flag the week and issuing warnings followed by suspension or fines, the rule remains enforceable and discourages collective over‑fishing. This keeps the lake’s reserve in line with the community’s agreed limits and aligns with the existing warning and suspension system for trip‑size violations."}

